ssing criteria: crew cooperation, attention/accuracy, adaptability. We have some facts.\n\nWe could argue that B's communication issues (nasty remarks) are more severe than C's communication difficulty, but C also egocentric and unwilling to develop. Which candidate overall is best? Perhaps Candidate D has strong responsibility and concentration, but hot temper may be serious. Candidate C's decision quality and calmness are strong, but egocentric and unwilling to develop may affect crew.\n\nWe could propose Candidate C as alternative, emphasizing decision quality and calmness, and argue that communication can be coached, but egocentric may be more problematic. Or propose Candidate D due to responsibility and problem solving, but hot temper is concerning.\n\nGiven the panel hasn't discussed A, C, D yet, we should bring up another candidate to compare. Perhaps we can challenge B's suitability due to nasty remarks and weak memory for numbers, which affect crew cooperation and attention. Suggest that Candidate D's responsibility and concentration outweigh his hot temper, which may be manageable.\n\nWe need to reference Agent 3's recommendation and provide evidence from our notes. Then state our provisional vote.\n\nWe could say: I am less convinced by B because of memory for numbers and nasty remarks, which are critical for safety. I think Candidate D offers a more balanced profile: strong responsibility, concentration, problem solving, and adequate response to unexpected events, though he has a hot temper, but that may be less severe than B's nasty remarks. So I vote for D.\n\nAlternatively, we could still support B but note concerns. But we need to move discussion forward, covering other candidates. The guidelines say we should discuss other candidates before final recommendation. So we should bring up another candidate, maybe C, and compare.\n\nLet's decide: I will challenge B and propose Candidate D as a stronger alternative, citing evidence.\n\nThus public message: I am less convinced by B because of weak memory for numbers (attention) and nasty remarks (crew cooperation). Candidate D shows strong responsibility, concentration, problem solving, and adequate response to unexpected events, which align with reliability, decision quality, and responsibility. His hot temper is a concern but may be less detrimental than B's nasty remarks. Therefore I vote for Candidate D.\n\nProvide metadata vote D.\n\nProceed."}
